Third-party components and data
-------------------------------

Project PLATEAU 3D city models (Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport
and Tourism, Japan). The tile used in this work is
`51324376_bldg_6697_op.gml` from the Hiroshima 2022 CityGML package
(`34100_hiroshima-shi_city_2022_citygml_3_op.zip`), obtained from the
G-spatial Information Center. The original CityGML is **not redistributed**
here: `code/fetch_plateau.py` downloads the single tile that is needed.
The web viewer does ship a **derived work**: `web/district.bin` contains a
triangle mesh of that tile (38,309 triangles) together with the computed
irradiance, published under the PLATEAU site policy with the attribution
shown on the page itself.
    https://www.mlit.go.jp/plateau/
    https://www.geospatial.jp/ckan/dataset/plateau-34100-hiroshima-shi-2022

Blender and Cycles (Blender Foundation), GNU GPL v2-or-later / Apache-2.0 for
parts of Cycles. Used as an external, unmodified binary to run the reference
path-traced control. No Blender code is included here.
    https://www.blender.org/

three.js (MIT License), loaded by the web viewer from a public CDN at run
time; no copy is included in this repository.
    https://threejs.org/

NumPy, PyTorch, Shapely, Matplotlib, Pillow and Segno, used through their
public APIs under their own licences (BSD-3-Clause, BSD-3-Clause,
BSD-3-Clause, PSF/BSD-style, MIT-CMU, BSD-3-Clause). Pillow writes the
animated GIFs and the video frames; Segno draws the QR codes on the
application PDF.

Solar geometry follows the NOAA Solar Calculator equations (public domain).
The clear-sky model follows Hottel (1976) and the Liu-Jordan diffuse
correlation; the sky dome follows Tregenza's 145-patch subdivision. These are
published methods, reimplemented here from their descriptions.
